//
// Expected: c0c0c0 — every query passes.
//
// The AEAD key is k2 = ENC(k1, m). The attacker is given k1, so it could open
// k2 if it ever held it, but building k2 needs m, which never leaves Alice.
// So x stays secret.
//
// This pins the distinction between opening a term and obtaining one.
// `obtainable` once accepted "this term is decomposable" as evidence that the
// attacker held it, so holding k1 was enough to credit the attacker with
// ENC(k1, m) and therefore with x. That reported an attack no Dolev-Yao
// attacker can mount, and it did so in the passive closure, where no
// substitution and no solver is involved.
//
// The self-contradiction is the tell, and it is what this model checks: the
// two queries below assert that the attacker obtains neither m nor k2, so a
// run that fails `confidentiality? x` claims a decryption under a key the same
// run certifies to be out of reach.
attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k1
	knows private m
	knows private x
	k2 = ENC(k1, m)
	c = AEAD_ENC(k2, x, nil)
	leaks k1
]

principal Bob[
	knows private k1
]

Alice -> Bob: c

queries[
	confidentiality? x
	confidentiality? m
	confidentiality? k2
]
